WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

1
00:00.200 --> 00:02.350
<v Instructor>In this lab, you will use Wireshark</v>

2
00:02.350 --> 00:04.770
to capture and inspect web traffic:

3
00:04.770 --> 00:07.280
Telnet traffic, and SSH traffic.

4
00:07.280 --> 00:10.080
Wireshark is a network protocol analyzer

5
00:10.080 --> 00:12.700
that lets you see what's happening on your network

6
00:12.700 --> 00:14.570
at a microscopic level.

7
00:14.570 --> 00:18.630
You can capture packets and store them for offline analysis.

8
00:18.630 --> 00:21.880
Wireshark includes many tools for deep inspection

9
00:21.880 --> 00:24.660
of hundreds of network protocols.

10
00:24.660 --> 00:28.733
Step one is open a terminal window in the CSE-LABVM.

11
00:30.212 --> 00:32.962
(mouse clicking)

12
00:36.800 --> 00:40.700
Step two is explore the Wireshark protocol analyzer.

13
00:40.700 --> 00:43.040
To capture traffic on your VM,

14
00:43.040 --> 00:46.710
you need to run Wireshark with escalated privileges.

15
00:46.710 --> 00:48.890
Enter the sudo wireshark command,

16
00:48.890 --> 00:51.600
and then enter password for the password.

17
00:51.600 --> 00:52.673
Wireshark will open.

18
01:04.080 --> 01:07.060
Under the listing of interfaces, we will be capturing

19
01:07.060 --> 01:09.470
traffic from both the ethernet interface

20
01:09.470 --> 01:12.288
and the loopback interface, so click any

21
01:12.288 --> 01:13.830
(mouse clicking)

22
01:13.830 --> 01:18.630
and then click Capture, Start.

23
01:18.630 --> 01:23.630
You can also click the Sharkfin icon on the toolbar.

24
01:24.112 --> 01:24.945
(mouse clicking)

25
01:24.945 --> 01:26.293
Open Firefox.

26
01:28.550 --> 01:32.163
In Firefox, enter cisco.com.

27
01:35.090 --> 01:40.090
Return to Wireshark, and click Capture, Stop.

28
01:40.260 --> 01:43.050
Alternatively, you can click the square button

29
01:43.050 --> 01:44.373
next to the Sharkfin.

30
01:45.890 --> 01:46.826
Close Firefox.

31
01:46.826 --> 01:49.940
(mouse clicking)

32
01:49.940 --> 01:53.120
In Wireshark, you will see the filter field

33
01:53.120 --> 01:56.313
and three key panes or work areas.

34
01:57.250 --> 02:00.490
The Apply a display filter field is

35
02:00.490 --> 02:02.460
directly below the toolbar.

36
02:02.460 --> 02:05.430
The packet list pane includes the following columns

37
02:05.430 --> 02:08.280
for each captured packet: Number,

38
02:08.280 --> 02:11.530
the number of packet and the numerical order,

39
02:11.530 --> 02:13.673
Time stamp for this packet,

40
02:14.520 --> 02:17.350
Source IP address for this packet,

41
02:17.350 --> 02:19.883
Destination IP address for this packet,

42
02:20.770 --> 02:25.690
the Protocol of this packet, the number of bytes captured

43
02:25.690 --> 02:30.090
for this packet, and any additional information.

44
02:30.090 --> 02:33.890
The packets detail pane shows the protocols

45
02:33.890 --> 02:37.110
and protocol fields of the selected packet.

46
02:37.110 --> 02:40.240
Notice that the fields can be expanded or collapsed

47
02:40.240 --> 02:43.083
by clicking on the arrow next to the field.

48
02:44.470 --> 02:47.530
The bytes pane shows the byte details

49
02:47.530 --> 02:48.840
of the selected packet.

50
02:48.840 --> 02:50.940
As you select parts of the packet

51
02:50.940 --> 02:53.060
in the packet details pane,

52
02:53.060 --> 02:55.530
the corresponding bytes will be highlighted

53
02:55.530 --> 02:56.700
in the packets pane.

54
02:56.700 --> 02:59.640
The left side shows the hexadecimal representation

55
02:59.640 --> 03:00.870
of the bytes.

56
03:00.870 --> 03:03.390
Step three is capture and analyze

57
03:03.390 --> 03:05.830
unencrypted Telnet traffic.

58
03:05.830 --> 03:08.093
In Wireshark, start a new capture.

59
03:10.530 --> 03:13.760
In the Unsaved packets dialog box,

60
03:13.760 --> 03:15.699
Click Continue without Saving.

61
03:15.699 --> 03:16.532
(mouse clicking)

62
03:16.532 --> 03:18.940
This will clear out the packets from your last capture

63
03:18.940 --> 03:20.480
and start a new capture.

64
03:20.480 --> 03:24.380
On your desktop, double click the Terminal icon

65
03:24.380 --> 03:26.093
to open a new terminal window.

66
03:27.940 --> 03:30.730
You can simulate a remote login to your VM

67
03:30.730 --> 03:34.580
by entering the telnet localhost command,

68
03:34.580 --> 03:38.513
and then logging in as cisco with password as the password.

69
03:41.920 --> 03:46.480
Enter exit to end this Telnet session.

70
03:46.480 --> 03:49.263
Return to Wireshark and stop the capture.

71
03:50.120 --> 03:52.670
In the Apply a display filter field,

72
03:52.670 --> 03:54.823
type telnet and press enter.

73
03:56.280 --> 03:59.860
On the toolbar, click the magnifying glass icon

74
03:59.860 --> 04:01.423
to find a packet.

75
04:02.340 --> 04:04.530
Additional search features are now shown

76
04:04.530 --> 04:07.030
below the Apply a display filter field.

77
04:07.030 --> 04:10.010
Click the arrows next to Display filter,

78
04:10.010 --> 04:12.101
and change to a String.

79
04:12.101 --> 04:14.100
(mouse clicking)

80
04:14.100 --> 04:16.150
Then click the arrows next to Packet list,

81
04:16.150 --> 04:18.669
and change it to Packet details.

82
04:18.669 --> 04:20.620
(mouse clicking)

83
04:20.620 --> 04:24.010
To find the packet requesting login information,

84
04:24.010 --> 04:26.297
type labvm login:.

85
04:29.250 --> 04:30.870
Click Find.

86
04:30.870 --> 04:31.960
(mouse clicking)

87
04:31.960 --> 04:33.520
Wireshark will highlight the packet

88
04:33.520 --> 04:36.220
that contains labvm login.

89
04:36.220 --> 04:39.100
You can tell that the text was sent unencrypted

90
04:39.100 --> 04:40.920
because you can read it.

91
04:40.920 --> 04:44.180
In the Packet list pane, click the highlighted packet

92
04:44.180 --> 04:48.410
with labvm login as the data to select it.

93
04:48.410 --> 04:51.610
To find the username and password, use your down arrow

94
04:51.610 --> 04:53.893
on the keyboard to select the next packet.

95
04:55.380 --> 04:57.740
In the Packet details pane, you should see

96
04:57.740 --> 05:00.470
the value for data under telnet is the first letter

97
05:00.470 --> 05:05.000
you typed in the field for the labvm login: prompt

98
05:05.005 --> 05:07.120
which was c for Cisco.

99
05:07.120 --> 05:08.750
If you click the down arrow again,

100
05:08.750 --> 05:11.900
you will see the next packet's data is also c

101
05:11.900 --> 05:15.235
because the source and destination of the same interface

102
05:15.235 --> 05:19.010
loopback 127.0.0.1.

103
05:19.010 --> 05:21.473
The packet is listed twice by Wireshark.

104
05:22.310 --> 05:24.260
Continue to press the down arrow key

105
05:24.260 --> 05:26.030
until you reach the last packet

106
05:26.030 --> 05:29.783
with a data value of o for the username Cisco.

107
05:32.230 --> 05:35.270
Click the down arrow for the next packet

108
05:35.270 --> 05:39.040
until you see the data password.

109
05:39.040 --> 05:41.060
Continue pressing the down arrow to read

110
05:41.060 --> 05:43.050
the data of the next eight packets

111
05:43.050 --> 05:45.350
which will reveal one letter at a time.

112
05:45.350 --> 05:49.300
That password is the password for the user Cisco.

113
05:49.300 --> 05:54.300
P-a-s-s-w-o-r-d.

114
05:55.580 --> 05:57.510
If you continue to press the down arrow

115
05:57.510 --> 05:59.900
through the rest of the captured packets,

116
05:59.900 --> 06:02.640
you will see all the texts sent and received

117
06:02.640 --> 06:06.260
during the Telnet session, including your exit command

118
06:06.260 --> 06:08.653
at the log out message.

119
06:10.030 --> 06:14.670
Step four is capture and analyze encrypted SSH traffic.

120
06:14.670 --> 06:16.023
Start a new capture.

121
06:17.085 --> 06:17.918
(mouse clicking)

122
06:17.918 --> 06:20.220
In the Unsaved packets dialog,

123
06:20.220 --> 06:22.193
click Continue without Saving.

124
06:23.460 --> 06:26.300
This will clear out the packets from your last capture

125
06:26.300 --> 06:27.980
and start a new capture.

126
06:27.980 --> 06:30.283
Return to your open Terminal window,

127
06:31.560 --> 06:32.950
(mouse clicking)

128
06:32.950 --> 06:36.140
and enter the command SSH localhost.

129
06:36.140 --> 06:38.490
If this is your first time to use the command,

130
06:38.490 --> 06:41.950
the system warns you about the authenticity of localhost

131
06:41.950 --> 06:44.410
and asks you if you want to continue.

132
06:44.410 --> 06:48.103
Enter yes, and then password as the password to log in.

133
06:50.560 --> 06:54.070
Enter exit to end the SSH session.

134
06:54.070 --> 06:56.399
Return to Wireshark and stop the Capture.

135
06:56.399 --> 06:58.360
(mouse clicking)

136
06:58.360 --> 07:00.530
If you left telnet as the search term

137
07:00.530 --> 07:02.940
in the Apply display filter field,

138
07:02.940 --> 07:04.800
no packets will be listed.

139
07:04.800 --> 07:07.743
Change the search term from telnet to ssh.

140
07:08.920 --> 07:12.650
All the packets from your SSH session should now be shown 

141
07:12.650 --> 07:14.860
in the packet list pane.

142
07:14.860 --> 07:19.120
In the packet details pane, expand the SSH Protocol fields

143
07:19.120 --> 07:20.423
to view the content.

144
07:23.480 --> 07:26.730
In the packet list pane, click the first packet.

145
07:26.730 --> 07:31.510
Use the down arrow to view a variety of SSH packets.

146
07:31.510 --> 07:34.230
Notice that the SSH Protocol field shows

147
07:34.230 --> 07:36.453
that all the data is encrypted.

